sec.t:
Spinosaurus was a huge meat-eater from North Africa, with a long crocodile-like snout and a tall sail on its back. Its first [[fossil|fossils]] were found in Egypt and described in 1915. They were destroyed when a museum in Munich was bombed during the [[@world-war-two|Second World War]].

New fossils from [[@morocco|Morocco]], described in 2014 and 2020, revived the debate. They showed short back legs, dense bones like a [[@penguin-flight|penguin's]] and a tall, flat tail like a paddle. One team argued Spinosaurus chased fish underwater, using its tail like a newt. In 2022, another team built a 3D model and argued it would have been unstable and slow in deep water. They think it waded in shallows like a giant heron, snatching fish. Both sides are still publishing new evidence.

{{story:penguins-cold}}

Nobody has drilled through the crust, so how do scientists measure it? The answer is [[@earthquakes|earthquakes]]. When the ground shakes, waves travel through the planet. They speed up or slow down depending on what rock they pass through.

In 1909, the Croatian scientist Andrija Mohorovičić studied waves from an earthquake near Zagreb. He noticed some waves arrived sooner than expected, as if they had taken a faster path through deeper rock. He had found the boundary between crust and mantle. It is now called the Mohorovičić discontinuity, or the Moho for short.

{{story:inside-earth}}

A tail keeps a kite from twisting and diving. It adds weight and drag at the bottom, so the kite points into the wind like a weathervane.

India's fighter kites, the *[[@pop-kites-patang|patang]]*, usually have no tail at all. That makes them unstable, but also quick to steer. A skilled flyer can make one dart, loop and dive with tiny tugs on the string, to cut down rival kites. The cutting strings, called *manja*, used to be coated with glass, but that harms birds, and glass-coated manja is now banned in India.

{{story:wind-blows}}

Older buildings were full of holes and ledges. Modern buildings, with smooth concrete, glass and sealed roofs, have almost none. Without a safe hole to nest in, sparrows can't breed.

Food has changed too. Adult sparrows eat seeds and grain, but their chicks need insects. Cities now have more concrete and neatly trimmed foreign plants, and fewer patches of wild weeds and native shrubs where insects live. Grain is now sold in sealed packets rather than in open sacks at the corner shop. Cats, crows and [[@air-pollution|air pollution]] may also play a part.

{{story:cities-grow}}

Gutters along the roof edge collect rain. A pipe carries it down to a filter, often a box or drum packed with layers of gravel, coarse sand and sometimes charcoal. A small device called a first-flush diverter can send the dirtiest water away before the rest is saved.

From the filter, water flows into a covered tank or into a recharge pit. A recharge pit is a hole a few metres deep, filled with stones and sand, that lets water seep slowly into the earth. Tanks must be kept tightly closed so mosquitoes can't breed in them.

{{story:water-cycle}}

In the early 1700s, a colour maker in Berlin was trying to make a red. Something went wrong with his ingredients, and he got a deep blue instead. It became known as Prussian blue, the first modern pigment made by chemistry. It was cheap, and soon painters everywhere were using it.

More discoveries followed in the 1800s and 1900s: bright yellows from chromium and cadmium, and later many colours made from oil-based chemicals. Today most paint colours are made in factories, which is why even a small school paint box can have dozens of bright shades.

{{story:inventions-accidents}}

[[foley|Foley]] is named after Jack Foley, who worked at Universal Studios in Hollywood from the late 1920s, when films first got sound. He found it was easier to act out sounds while watching the film than to record them on set.

Foley studios look like junk shops. They have patches of floor made of gravel, wood, tiles and grass, plus shelves of shoes, chains, keys and old phones. Artists walk on the spot in the right shoes, matching every step of the actor on screen. A good Foley artist can make you believe someone is walking on ice, in high heels, while carrying shopping.

{{story:sound-travel}}

India's oldest museum is the Indian Museum in [[@kolkata|Kolkata]], which grew out of the collection of the Asiatic Society in 1814. It holds fossils, an Egyptian [[@mummies|mummy]], coins, and carved railings from an ancient Buddhist [[stupa|stupa]] at Bharhut. Mumbai's Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Vastu Sangrahalaya, once called the Prince of Wales Museum, opened in 1922. The National Museum in New Delhi was set up after independence, in 1949.

Many smaller museums tell more local stories. [[@ahmedabad|Ahmedabad]] has a museum of kites, Delhi has a National Rail Museum full of old steam engines, and Bhopal has a museum of tribal life built with the help of Adivasi artists.

{{story:archaeology}}

Qawwali traces its roots to Delhi around 700 years ago, during the time of the [[@delhi-sultanate|Delhi Sultanate]]. The poet and musician Amir Khusrau was a devoted follower of the Sufi saint Nizamuddin Auliya. Tradition says Khusrau blended Persian, Arabic and Indian music into a new style, and wrote many songs still sung today, such as "Chhap Tilak".

Historians point out that Sufis had held music gatherings, called *sama*, before Khusrau's time. So qawwali probably grew slowly, with Khusrau as one of its most important shapers. Either way, Nizamuddin's shrine in [[@delhi|Delhi]] is still one of the best places to hear it, especially on Thursday evenings.

{{story:delhi-sultanate}}

[[@ammonites|Ammonites]], relatives of squid that lived alongside the dinosaurs, built coiled shells too. So does the living nautilus, a [[cephalopod|cephalopod]] of the Indian and Pacific Oceans. Its shell is split into chambers, and the animal lives only in the newest, biggest one. It can float or sink by changing the gas and liquid in the older rooms.

{{story:ammonites}}

The same idea of growing at the edge shows up in sunflower heads and pine cones, which line up with the [[@fibonacci-nature|Fibonacci numbers]]. Nothing magical is going on. Anything that builds by adding new parts beside old ones tends to end up as a spiral.

Write a 1 at the top. Below, write 1 1. Each new row starts and ends with 1, and every other number is the sum of the two numbers just above it. You get 1 2 1, then 1 3 3 1, then 1 4 6 4 1.

This triangle was studied in India, where a 10th-century commentary on Pingala describes it as the Meru Prastara, named after a mythical mountain. Chinese mathematicians such as Yang Hui wrote about it in the 1200s, and French thinker Blaise Pascal studied it in the 1650s. Hidden inside are the counting numbers, the [[@triangular-numbers|triangular numbers]], powers of two and even the Fibonacci numbers.

{{story:rangoli-symmetry}}

Healthy cartilage soaked in synovial fluid is extremely slippery, with less [[@friction|friction]] than most man-made bearings. That's how a knee can bend millions of times over a lifetime.

Cartilage has no blood supply, though, so it heals slowly if damaged. Over many years, especially after injuries or with heavy wear, it can thin. That is one cause of arthritis, which makes joints stiff and sore. Doctors can replace a badly worn hip or knee with a metal and plastic one. The British [[surgeon|surgeon]] John Charnley developed a successful hip replacement in the 1960s, and today it is one of the most common operations in the world.

{{story:robots}}

[[@gravity-fall|Gravity]] pulls the booster down at enormous speed. In the last half-minute, one engine lights for the landing burn. Computers measure height and speed many times a second and swivel the engine to keep the tall, thin rocket upright, like balancing a pencil on your palm.

The engine can't throttle low enough to hover, so the timing has to be exact. The booster reaches zero speed at the very moment it touches the pad. When there isn't enough fuel to fly back to land, the booster lands instead on a robot ship waiting hundreds of kilometres out at sea.

{{story:parachutes}}

Patan was the capital of the Chaulukya, or Solanki, kings. Historians believe Queen Udayamati built Rani ki Vav in memory of her husband, King Bhimadeva I.

It was designed like an upside-down temple. As you go down, you pass through pillared halls on each level. The walls get richer in carvings the deeper you go, as if you are walking towards something holy. Water itself was seen as sacred.

{{story:stepwells}}

Cold has always been the gentlest way to keep food. In winter, people in New England cut huge blocks of ice from frozen lakes and stored them in sawdust in [[@frozen-food|ice houses]].

In 1833 a [[@boston|Boston]] businessman named Frederic Tudor sent a shipload of this ice all the way to [[@kolkata|Kolkata]], then called Calcutta. The voyage took about four months. Much of the ice survived the trip, and for years afterwards American ice was sold in the city.

{{story:refrigerator}}
